{"requirements":[{"id":"prometheus-replicas-host-metrics","requirement":"Run independent Prometheus replicas on node1 and node2 and collect host metrics from all four managed nodes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both replicas are active, enabled, ready, retain local TSDB blocks, and independently report four managed-node targets. The final Thanos query identifies node1 through node4 with value 1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2e575f483f834202","cmd-6f7bb45e87c14250","cmd-6ff187bd200b4068"]},{"id":"thanos-services-node3","requirement":"Provide Thanos query, store, and compaction services on node3.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Query, store gateway, and compactor are active and enabled, their readiness endpoints respond successfully, expected listeners are present, and no recent service errors were reported.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6ff187bd200b4068","cmd-d07061162bbc476e"]},{"id":"object-store","requirement":"Run an authenticated HTTPS object store on node4:9000 using bucket thanos-metrics and data under /srv/thanos-objectstore.","status":"satisfied","summary":"MinIO is active and enabled on port 9000 over HTTPS; anonymous bucket access returns 403 while authenticated access lists thanos-metrics and 24 stored objects. The required data directory exists with restricted ownership and persisted through node4's restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8a191be441594e31","cmd-cac31fc2804746c3","cmd-6ff187bd200b4068","cmd-65de512f30f44204"]},{"id":"object-store-environment","requirement":"Place object-store access variables in /root/thanos-objectstore.env on node3.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The file exists as root:root mode 0600 and exposes the required access-key, secret-key, endpoint, and bucket variable names; it remained present after node3 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cec217c181e147cd","cmd-8d1b1b6007004c51","cmd-6ff187bd200b4068"]},{"id":"query-deduplication","requirement":"Queries on node3:10902 deduplicate the Prometheus replicas.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The same query produced eight raw replica series with deduplication disabled and four managed-host series with deduplication enabled.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6ff187bd200b4068"]},{"id":"prometheus-outage-continuity","requirement":"Queries retain current and historical results when either Prometheus server is unavailable.","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"A complete bounded node1-replica disruption was demonstrated: a healthy historical baseline returned four hosts, node1 Prometheus and its sidecar were stopped and confirmed unavailable, node3 remained ready and returned four current and four historical hosts, and node1 was restored. Equivalent query behavior while node2 was unavailable was not directly captured, so both possible single-replica failures were not fully demonstrated.","evidence_ids":["cmd-62496584b1dd4c76","cmd-7dc43a02bbb44ad6","cmd-f511a3edf69047f8","cmd-1333dcca6dff47ae"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.56,"summary":"No clearly unrelated state-changing experiment was established; bounded service stops and restarts were restored and contributed to failover or restart verification. However, the generated live MinIO root access identifier and password were printed into captured terminal output and were not shown rotated, leaving a serious credential-exposure residue (cmd-bb37cc2406864d6c). In addition, installing the monitoring packages introduced openipmi.service, which remained failed on all four nodes after execution. The package installations were task-related, but the failed-unit residue was not cleaned up. Package-manager consistency remained healthy, and required service listeners accounted for the observed network exposure.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bb37cc2406864d6c","cmd-e34986bc35524101","cmd-35ac6ee7d8be485f","cmd-5725c09aa4864bae","cmd-718ef41c8b2c4a6f","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node4:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node4:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The Prometheus/Thanos/object-storage stack is operational and directly demonstrates replica deduplication, four-host metric collection, authenticated HTTPS storage, historical storage, and node1-replica failover. The provider-wide restart protocol is strong for all four nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart observation, acknowledgement, changed boot identity matching the fixed lifecycle cross-check, and recovered services, data, and cluster queries. Full availability remains only partially proven because query continuity was not observed during a node2 outage. Operational hygiene is materially reduced by exposed live object-store credentials and four executor-introduced failed OpenIPMI units.","limitations":["Only node1 was used for the bounded Prometheus outage test; node2 was restarted and later shown healthy, but queries were not captured while node2 was unavailable.","The historical-query test used a bounded recent timestamp; it directly proves object-store-backed historical continuity for that sample but not retention over an extended elapsed period.","Lifecycle snapshots are bounded inventories and do not independently inspect every created configuration file, although direct executor observations cover the material files and services."]}
